If you want to check whether a class is a custom exception using an annotation in Java, you’ll need to combine two things:
A custom annotation
Reflection to inspect the class
1. Create a custom annotation
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target(ElementType.TYPE)
public @interface CustomException {
}
2. Use the annotation on your exception classes
@CustomException
public class MyException extends Exception {
}

@CustomException
public class AnotherException extends RuntimeException {
}
3. Write a method to check it

You can verify both:

whether it’s an exception (Throwable subclass)
whether it has your annotation
public class ExceptionChecker {

    public static boolean isCustomException(Class<?> clazz) {
        return Throwable.class.isAssignableFrom(clazz) &&
               clazz.isAnnotationPresent(CustomException.class);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isCustomException(MyException.class));       // true
        System.out.println(isCustomException(String.class));           // false
    }
}
Key idea
Throwable.class.isAssignableFrom(clazz) → checks if it's an exception
clazz.isAnnotationPresent(...) → checks your custom marker
