In microservices, failures are common due to network issues or service downtime, so we handle them using multiple resilience patterns.

In my project, I used Resilience4j to implement a Circuit Breaker pattern. The circuit breaker monitors service calls and moves through states like closed, open, and half-open.

If a service fails repeatedly beyond a threshold, the circuit opens and stops further calls to prevent cascading failures. During this time, we return a fallback response, like a default value or cached data.

In addition to circuit breaker, we also configure:

Retry mechanism for transient failures
Timeouts to avoid long waits
Fallback methods to degrade gracefully

This ensures the system remains stable even if one service is down.
